"Folds equal-sized positive and negative value bands onto a compact time strip using ordered color intensity.\n\nUse when: Many long time series need compact comparison of deviations. Readers can learn the band size and sign color conventions.\n\nAvoid when: Exact point values or an unfamiliar audience require immediate direct reading.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes time. Color family encodes sign, intensity encodes band, and residual strip height encodes within-band magnitude.\n\nBuild: Choose a meaningful reference and subtract it from the series. Partition deviations into equal-magnitude bands and declare the band size. Fold bands into one strip with a readable sign-and-intensity legend.\n\nCheck: Check band boundaries are equally spaced in original measurement units. Verify sign reflection and folding preserve recoverable magnitudes. Use identical band sizes across strips intended for direct comparison.\n\nPitfalls: Folded bands are deliberately less direct than a normal axis and require explanation. Do not treat darkest color as a missing value or change band sizes silently across rows.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic hourly sensor deviations across twenty devices with a shared 5-unit band size.\n\nAlternatives: Lasagna plot, Line chart, Diverging bar chart."
